# Checkpoints 2 and 4 - parallel-beam redundancy, and the sign convention.
# A ring bar at phantom-frame angle phi, radius 60mm, has ray offset
#     d(phi) = | 60*sin(phi + theta) - t |
# so it is cut dead centre when 60*sin(phi+theta) = t. At |t| = 60mm that picks
# out exactly ONE bar: the central Al bar is 60mm off the ray and the baseplate
# is 75mm below it in z. Steel bars at phi = 0,120,240 (r=10mm); poly at
# 60,180,300 (r=15mm).
#
# CHECKPOINT 4 - the sign table. Which MATERIAL is isolated depends on signs:
#     theta    t       bar cut    material  chord   EXPECT T ~
#     +30    +60mm     phi= 60    poly      30mm      0.79
#     -30    +60mm     phi=120    steel     20mm      0.32
#     +30    -60mm     phi=240    steel     20mm      0.32
#     -30    -60mm     phi=300    poly      30mm      0.79
# Factor 2.5 between poly and steel - unmissable. Pattern backwards means theta
# and t disagree in sign.
# HONEST LIMIT: a simultaneous flip of BOTH signs is undetectable - pipe, bars
# and slab are all mirror-symmetric about the beam axis, so (theta,t) ->
# (-theta,-t) is an exact symmetry of the object. Relative sign is measurable;
# absolute convention is fixed by doc only, and bites from Phase 3 on.
#
# CHECKPOINT 2 - redundancy. p(theta,t) = p(theta+180, -t), NOT p(theta+180, t)
# - the detector coordinate reverses, and assuming otherwise is exactly how a
# sinogram ends up flipped. The last run is the same physical line as the first.
/run/verbose      0
/event/verbose    0
/tracking/verbose 0
/cttwin/phantom bars
/run/initialize
/cttwin/scan/angle        30 deg
/cttwin/scan/translation  60 mm
/run/beamOn 100000
/cttwin/scan/angle       -30 deg
/cttwin/scan/translation  60 mm
/run/beamOn 100000
/cttwin/scan/angle        30 deg
/cttwin/scan/translation -60 mm
/run/beamOn 100000
/cttwin/scan/angle       -30 deg
/cttwin/scan/translation -60 mm
/run/beamOn 100000
/cttwin/scan/angle       210 deg
/cttwin/scan/translation -60 mm
/run/beamOn 100000
